      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4.5y-1.5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-2.5x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $3xy$ term).",
